WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.469
This is CNN breaking news.

00:00:03.903 --> 00:00:05.238
And we are following more breaking news

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:06.573
out of Tokyo this morning.

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:07.374
And Japan Airlines

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:08.108
passenger jet

00:00:08.108 --> 00:00:10.310
with nearly 400 people on board.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:11.478
See it landing there

00:00:11.478 --> 00:00:12.912
with a fireball on its wing

00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:14.848
collided with the Japan Coast Guard

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.082
aircraft on its way

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:18.318
to help with earthquake relief efforts.

00:00:18.318 --> 00:00:19.719
Japan's public broadcaster

00:00:19.719 --> 00:00:20.487
now reporting

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:22.155
five crew members on that Coast

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:23.423
Guard plane had been killed.

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:25.825
The captain is in critical condition now.

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:27.627
Video shows the commercial plane

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:29.863
igniting into a fireball

00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:32.132
barreling down the runway this morning.

00:00:32.132 --> 00:00:34.300
The airline says nearly everyone from

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.336
that flight was safely evacuated.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:38.104
Japan's public broadcaster

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:40.373
says 17 passengers were injured

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.079
and this was the panicked scene

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:47.347
inside the plane

00:00:47.347 --> 00:00:48.214
as smoke filled

00:00:48.214 --> 00:00:49.616
the cabin and passengers

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:51.551
scrambled to find a way out.

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:53.219
CNN's Will Ripley joins us

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:55.055
live from Tokyo.

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:55.655
And Will,

00:00:55.655 --> 00:00:57.991
I just want to talk about the crew.

00:00:57.991 --> 00:00:58.425
Actually,

00:00:58.425 --> 00:01:00.927
we'll get to the evacuation efforts,

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:02.095
but the crew who died

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:03.630
they were on their way to help.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:04.831
Correct. Can you give us more

00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:08.034
Yes.

00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:08.802
This side,

00:01:08.802 --> 00:01:09.769
Haneda Airport is right

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:11.137
in the heart of Tokyo, about 20 minutes

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:12.172
from where I'm standing right now.

00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:13.173
And there's also a Coast

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:15.008
Guard base nearby.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:16.176
The whole airport actually sits

00:01:16.176 --> 00:01:16.843
right on the bay.

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:18.378
It's a beautiful approach

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:19.245
when you fly in there.

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:20.713
It's probably my favorite airport

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:21.648
when I fly in to Tokyo.

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:22.916
My cameraman, John,

00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:23.283
flew in there

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:25.785
just a few hours before this happened.

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:27.087
But in this case, this

00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:28.822
this Coast Guard aircraft

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:31.224
somehow ended up on the same runway

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:32.625
as this.

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:33.793
Japan Airlines

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:35.895
jet was flying in a packed plane

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:36.796
coming from the tourist

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:39.332
hotspot of Sapporo in northern Japan.

00:01:39.466 --> 00:01:40.433
And these two planes

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:42.969
had an incredibly fiery collision.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.172
We know that according to NHK, Japan's

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:48.208
national broadcaster,

00:01:48.208 --> 00:01:49.742
five crew members were killed.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:52.212
The captain was able to escape,

00:01:52.212 --> 00:01:54.747
but he is currently being held,

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:57.150
are currently on life support.

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:57.851
I've also lost

00:01:57.851 --> 00:01:58.718
my IFB signal,

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:00.153
so let's not ask a follow up question

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:01.754
until I get reconnected.

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:03.656
But but what we know is that this

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:05.525
this Coast Guard plane

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:07.127
was headed to assist

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:08.094
with the earthquake relief

00:02:08.094 --> 00:02:08.695
efforts, that

00:02:08.695 --> 00:02:09.929
within hours of the new year,

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:11.965
there was a 7.5 magnitude quake

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:13.967
that rocked to the central western

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:14.968
coast of Japan.

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:16.402
I know we're going to talk more

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:17.604
about that in a moment.

00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:18.805
There's the number of dead

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:20.440
in the dozens right now.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:21.975
But what is truly extraordinary

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:22.976
is that what could have been

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:24.110
a horrific mass

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:27.147
casualty situation was was averted

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.815
because in a matter of seconds,

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:30.583
despite the fact that passengers

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:32.552
say the rear and middle exits of this

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:34.487
aircraft were damaged in this collision,

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:35.688
there was a fire rapidly

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:36.856
engulfing the plane.

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:40.460
And yet everybody 367 passengers, eight

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:43.463
children under the age of two, included

00:02:43.463 --> 00:02:44.397
on the passenger list.

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:45.732
So a lot of parents there,

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:45.999
you know,

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:47.700
clutching onto their young babies.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:48.801
And these 12 crew members,

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:50.470
they all were able to make it off

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.138
of that aircraft safely.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:53.873
All right.

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:55.141
Will Ripley, we do have follow up

00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:56.176
questions we're going to ask you

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:56.776
when you're plugged

00:02:56.776 --> 00:02:58.077
back in over the course

00:02:58.077 --> 00:02:58.578
the next hour

00:02:58.578 --> 00:02:59.946
as we keep an eye on the breaking news

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:00.813
from on the ground.

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:01.614
In the meantime,

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:02.448
former FAA

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.817
safety inspector and CNN Safety analyst

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:06.286
David Soucie is here.

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:06.986
So first,

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:07.720
walk us through

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.856
what happens in an emergency situation

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:10.823
like this on the runway,

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:12.492
because there was not a mass

00:03:12.492 --> 00:03:14.427
casualty event, event right.

00:03:14.427 --> 00:03:17.864
Essentially, this is when things go well.

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:19.465
It's truly miraculous in the fact

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:20.333
that there were two exits

00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:22.535
actually blocked and not able to be used.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:25.338
Really, really made this miraculous event

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:26.973
to be able to get everybody off of there.

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.341
So what happens at this point

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:29.475
is everybody

00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:31.744
they deployed the slides

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:33.513
so that everybody could get out quickly

00:03:33.513 --> 00:03:35.415
and it really is amazing that

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:36.482
that many people got out of there

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:37.317
so quickly.

00:03:37.317 --> 00:03:38.351
They they

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:39.452
you channel out,

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:40.653
you go to your nearest exit,

00:03:40.653 --> 00:03:41.354
as they always tell you

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:42.488
when you get on the airplane,

00:03:42.488 --> 00:03:43.590
look for your nearest exit.

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:44.757
It may be behind you.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:45.725
This is what they did.

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:46.426
They were aware.

00:03:46.426 --> 00:03:47.694
They were they were conscious.

00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:49.862
They were ready for any kind of accident.

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.931
And this crew is amazing

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:53.199
that they got everybody out of there.

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:54.067
So quickly

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:55.468
by just channeling them through the

00:03:55.468 --> 00:03:56.869
through through the aisles.

00:03:56.869 --> 00:03:57.804
There's already smoke.

00:03:57.804 --> 00:03:59.472
If you can visualize this, there's

00:03:59.472 --> 00:04:01.274
smoke in the cabin at the time.

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:03.243
There may be fire going on at the time.

00:04:03.243 --> 00:04:04.110
People are

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:05.345
getting out these exits

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:06.546
and jumping onto the slides

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:08.548
and coming out to the out to the ramp.

00:04:08.548 --> 00:04:10.049
And then once they clear that,

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:11.317
they have to get away.

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:13.586
So this is all an incredibly

00:04:13.586 --> 00:04:14.854
urgent situation.

00:04:14.854 --> 00:04:16.356
And to have someone be it

00:04:16.356 --> 00:04:16.623
to be

00:04:16.623 --> 00:04:17.657
able to manage it, a crew

00:04:17.657 --> 00:04:18.558
to be able to manage it

00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:20.660
and get them all out of there safely

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:22.095
is really testament to the work

00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:22.562
that they did

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:25.164
to prepare for this type of tragedy.

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:27.467
David, to that point,

00:04:27.467 --> 00:04:28.434
there is preparation,

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:29.269
then there's action

00:04:29.269 --> 00:04:31.437
and the former you can do

00:04:31.437 --> 00:04:32.405
whatever you want in the lead

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:34.374
up to the ladder is what really matters.

00:04:34.374 --> 00:04:35.642
And when you look at the pictures

00:04:35.642 --> 00:04:36.643
from the landing

00:04:36.643 --> 00:04:38.144
where the wing was clearly on fire

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:40.246
to just how quickly the entire flame

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:42.448
or the entire plane was engulfed

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:43.716
you see it there.

00:04:43.716 --> 00:04:45.885
It's was cracked and half the flames

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:47.520
crawling up the windows.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:48.688
How much of a time when

00:04:48.688 --> 00:04:49.622
did they have here

00:04:51.858 --> 00:04:53.059
It's it's seconds.

00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:53.426
I mean,

00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:55.628
it just within about 2 minutes,

00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:58.298
everybody was up and out of their seats

00:04:58.298 --> 00:05:00.600
and starting to exit.

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.368
Here's the issue with this.

00:05:02.368 --> 00:05:03.503
And the reason that it came

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:04.470
so quickly

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.638
is the fact that it was

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:07.540
a solid enough hit,

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:08.541
a collision

00:05:08.541 --> 00:05:10.677
that not only the tip of the wing head,

00:05:10.677 --> 00:05:12.145
but the structure of the spa.

00:05:12.145 --> 00:05:14.113
The main wing spa is correct.

00:05:14.113 --> 00:05:15.281
You can tell that that happened

00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:16.816
because the fuel cells go through there

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.584
and the connections to the fuel cells

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:19.652
are in the fuselage

00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:20.720
of the aircraft underneath

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:21.988
where people are setting.

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:23.022
So when that hit

00:05:23.022 --> 00:05:24.457
and it pulled that wing back,

00:05:24.457 --> 00:05:26.426
it obviously ruptured some fuel cells

00:05:26.426 --> 00:05:28.061
or at least some fuel attachments,

00:05:28.061 --> 00:05:29.062
and that started pouring

00:05:29.062 --> 00:05:31.130
fuel into the area.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:32.465
Combine that with some winds

00:05:32.465 --> 00:05:34.100
that were going on at the time, too.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:35.401
And that can spread the fire

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:36.669
much more rapidly.

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:38.571
So we are talking about

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:39.639
just seconds to be able

00:05:39.639 --> 00:05:41.941
to react, to be able to get out of there.

00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:43.509
Leave your personal belongings

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:44.277
behind

00:05:44.277 --> 00:05:45.445
and get out of that airplane

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:47.180
as quickly as possible.

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.082
David, as a safety inspector,

00:05:49.082 --> 00:05:50.116
former safety inspector,

00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:51.217
can you talk about

00:05:51.217 --> 00:05:52.185
what are the next steps

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:53.920
in terms of an investigation?

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.555
What are the things that people will

00:05:55.555 --> 00:05:56.489
start to look at?

00:05:58.424 --> 00:05:58.825
Well, there's

00:05:58.825 --> 00:05:59.792
some things that you can jump

00:05:59.792 --> 00:06:00.927
to conclusions about.

00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:01.661
And we want to make sure

00:06:01.661 --> 00:06:03.629
as an investigator, you don't do that.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:05.965
We need to look first at what happened.

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:07.633
And it's too early to assign

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:09.402
blame to anybody or anything like that.

00:06:09.402 --> 00:06:11.037
But what we can conclude

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:13.473
is that we know that the Coast Guard was

00:06:13.606 --> 00:06:15.041
was heading out to

00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:16.275
to provide relief

00:06:16.275 --> 00:06:17.477
to the earthquake victims.

00:06:17.477 --> 00:06:18.511
We know that they were in a hurry

00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:19.746
to get out and do that.

00:06:19.746 --> 00:06:20.680
They were moving.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.415
The if you look at how busy

00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:23.649
that airport is,

00:06:23.649 --> 00:06:26.085
that's a very, very active airport.

00:06:26.252 --> 00:06:27.987
There's a lot of moving pieces

00:06:27.987 --> 00:06:29.222
and we talk about this a lot.

00:06:29.222 --> 00:06:30.056
In the United States,

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:32.458
we've had a really large increase

00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:33.659
in the number of near-misses

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:36.496
and runway incursions such as this one.

00:06:37.397 --> 00:06:39.365
What we haven't had a fatality.

00:06:39.365 --> 00:06:41.134
We haven't had anything like this one

00:06:41.134 --> 00:06:42.435
so far in the United States.

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:44.604
But the FAA has started a panel

00:06:44.604 --> 00:06:45.872
to investigate

00:06:45.872 --> 00:06:47.240
these types of incidents

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:47.940
where I would start

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.709
with this investigation first

00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:51.577
is the communications,

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:52.845
the communications between

00:06:52.845 --> 00:06:54.347
who said what to whom.

00:06:54.347 --> 00:06:55.081
And as you know,

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:56.949
there's been a lot in the United States.

00:06:56.949 --> 00:06:57.483
One of them

00:06:57.483 --> 00:06:58.651
most recently

00:06:58.651 --> 00:06:59.952
was where the

00:06:59.952 --> 00:07:01.621
air traffic controller had said,

00:07:01.621 --> 00:07:03.756
go ahead and go on to runway 17 left

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:05.825
or I don't know exactly which one it was.

00:07:05.825 --> 00:07:06.692
And when the pilot

00:07:06.692 --> 00:07:08.795
repeated that back to confirm,

00:07:08.795 --> 00:07:11.631
he said something different, like 17.

00:07:11.631 --> 00:07:12.365
Right.

00:07:12.365 --> 00:07:13.232
And the air

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:14.801
traffic controller was busy,

00:07:14.801 --> 00:07:16.836
fatigued, had worked several shifts

00:07:17.036 --> 00:07:17.870
and then

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:18.504
said that

00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:19.739
it didn't recognize the fact

00:07:19.739 --> 00:07:21.541
that he didn't get the message properly.

00:07:21.541 --> 00:07:22.975
And then that airplane taxied

00:07:22.975 --> 00:07:24.343
on to an active live runway

00:07:24.343 --> 00:07:25.711
where there was an airplane landing.

00:07:25.711 --> 00:07:27.480
So these types of communications

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:27.947
are something

00:07:27.947 --> 00:07:30.450
that's under close scrutiny right now

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:32.752
with the FAA in the United States

00:07:32.952 --> 00:07:35.354
and also in Europe.

00:07:35.354 --> 00:07:36.956
And Japan.

00:07:36.956 --> 00:07:37.957
We have to really

00:07:37.957 --> 00:07:39.559
look at how this is happening

00:07:39.559 --> 00:07:41.093
as these airports get busier

00:07:41.093 --> 00:07:42.795
and busier and busier.

00:07:42.795 --> 00:07:44.230
You still have a human being

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:45.298
trying to figure out

00:07:45.298 --> 00:07:46.632
what I call the fourth dimension.

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:48.868
It's not only where the airplanes are,

00:07:49.001 --> 00:07:50.336
but where will they be

00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:51.504
if they make a mistake

00:07:51.504 --> 00:07:53.105
or if they even if they do

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:54.140
what they're supposed to do,

00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:56.042
where will they be in the future?

00:07:56.042 --> 00:07:57.009
And do those times

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.411
intersect with each other?

00:07:58.411 --> 00:07:59.178
And so it's an

00:07:59.178 --> 00:08:01.581
extremely difficult job, and

00:08:02.715 --> 00:08:04.183
I would hope that it improves.

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:05.351
We really have a lot of efforts

00:08:05.351 --> 00:08:06.552
going forward to improve that

00:08:06.552 --> 00:08:08.321
over the next year or two

00:08:08.321 --> 00:08:09.622
with the FAA at least,

00:08:09.622 --> 00:08:12.058
and in the international community.

00:08:12.058 --> 00:08:13.392
David, thanks for this insight.

